// The Apple Developer team everything ships under: Humble Solutions Private
// Limited. Committed rather than set per developer, because there is one team
// and one App ID — a per-developer value is what made the bundle identifier
// unregisterable in the first place (see below).
//
// Not a secret: a team id is embedded in every shipped app. CI needs none of
// it, building with CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO.
TEAM_ID=53GDRR35QU

PRODUCT_NAME=FinAI

// Fixed, and deliberately NOT suffixed with $(TEAM_ID).
//
// The project template appended the team id so two developers could install
// side by side. But the bundle identifier is the value Apple registers Sign in
// with Apple against, and the value Supabase validates a native Apple ID token
// against — so one that changes per developer cannot be registered at all, and
// every registration would break the moment TEAM_ID was filled in (#16).
//
// Matches the Android applicationId, so both platforms are one identity.
PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER=com.humblesolutions.finai

// The iOS Google client id, reversed, which Google's SDK requires as a URL
// scheme (see Info.plist). Set per developer in Config.local.xcconfig, which
// is gitignored and included at the bottom of this file - copy
// Config.local.xcconfig.example. It ships inside every copy of the app, so it
// is not a secret, but like the forward id in local.properties it stays out of
// git. Empty here, so a fresh checkout builds with Google reported as not
// configured instead of failing.
GOOGLE_REVERSED_CLIENT_ID=

CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION=1
MARKETING_VERSION=1.0

// Sign in with Apple needs an entitlement, and the entitlement needs to be
// declared somewhere the project reads. Set here rather than in project.pbxproj
// so the file stays hand-editable.
CODE_SIGN_ENTITLEMENTS=iosApp/iosApp.entitlements

// Last, so a developer's values override the defaults above. `#include?`
// rather than `#include`: a checkout without the file still builds.
#include? "Config.local.xcconfig"
